🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗿𝗲𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗖𝗮𝗻'𝘁 𝗬𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝗹𝘃𝗲 𝗗𝗡𝗦?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your application Pods cannot resolve Kubernetes Service names, but the Pods themselves are Running. How will you troubleshoot the DNS issue?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately restart CoreDNS. First, I'd determine whether the issue affects one Pod, one namespace, or the entire cluster.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Test DNS from the affected Pod

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- nslookup kubernetes.default

If "nslookup" is unavailable, use a suitable debugging container.

2️⃣ Check the Pod's DNS configuration

kubectl exec <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- cat /etc/resolv.conf

Verify the nameserver and search domains.

3️⃣ Check CoreDNS Pods

kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

Look for unhealthy Pods, DNS errors, or upstream resolution failures.

4️⃣ Check the DNS Service

kubectl get svc -n kube-system
kubectl get endpoints kube-dns -n kube-system

Verify the DNS Service and its backend endpoints.

5️⃣ Check CoreDNS configuration

kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

Review the Corefile for incorrect configuration or recent changes.

6️⃣ Investigate networking

Check NetworkPolicies, CNI health, and connectivity to the DNS Service on UDP/TCP port 53.

Also verify whether external DNS names fail while internal Kubernetes Service names work.

7️⃣ Verify the fix

Retest internal and external name resolution from the affected workloads, then monitor CoreDNS health and application connectivity.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Pods Running ≠ DNS Working.

Follow the troubleshooting path:

Affected Pod → DNS Configuration → CoreDNS → DNS Service → Network → Root Cause

Identify the failing layer before making production changes.
